"Let $A\\subset\\mathbb{N}$, and let $\\overline{\\mathsf d}(A)$ and $\\underline{\\mathsf d}(A)$ denote its upper and lower densities.\n\n**Density threshold conjecture.** The following assertions hold:\n\n1. If $\\overline{\\mathsf d}(A)>2/3$, then there is an infinite set $B\\subset\\mathbb{N}$ such that $B+B\\subset A$.\n2. If $\\underline{\\mathsf d}(A)>1/2$, then there is an infinite set $B\\subset\\mathbb{N}$ such that $B+B\\subset A$.\n\nExamples in the paper have upper density arbitrarily close to $2/3$ and lower density arbitrarily close to $1/2$ while avoiding such configurations, motivating the proposed sharp thresholds."